  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 205,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex X. Reforming the Fiscal Framework in Mexico: Freeing\nFiscal Policy to Respond to Shocks\nWith fiscal contingencies all but depleted following the authorities’ response to the pandemic in 2020,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 205,
      "end_char": 402,
      "text": "limited space in the 2022 and 2023 budgets, and mounting downside risks for 2023, the Mexican\nauthorities have proposed reforms to the fiscal framework to enable the replenishment of their buffers\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 402,
      "end_char": 613,
      "text": "for 2023. The proposals represent a quick and efficient repurposing of resources in light of immediate\nrisks but increase complexity while doing little to improve reliability and tackle the pro-cyclical bias in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 613,
      "end_char": 788,
      "text": "fiscal policy. Comprehensive reforms aimed at addressing issues with the current framework will\ninevitably take time but could build on staff advice.\nA. Background and Issues\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 788,
      "end_char": 972,
      "text": "1. Mexico’s fiscal contingencies are low as downside risks to the global economy are\nrising. In responding to the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020, Mexico used its fiscal buffers as revenues\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 972,
      "end_char": 1165,
      "text": "fell short of budget projections, transferring 0.9 percent of GDP from stabilization funds to the\nbudget. This transfer and the previous one of 0.5 percent of GDP in 2019 almost depleted these\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1165,
      "end_char": 1366,
      "text": "funds, leaving a remaining balance of 0.2 percent of GDP. While recourse to fiscal contingencies has\nnot been necessary since, increased spending pressures relative to the budget mean that oil revenue\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1366,
      "end_char": 1561,
      "text": "surprises in 2022 are unlikely to be large enough to allow for replenishing the stabilization funds.\nWith downside risks to the global and Mexican economies rising, a key issue is how Mexico can\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1561,
      "end_char": 1797,
      "text": "rebuild its capacity to respond to future shocks.\n2. Two funds form the basis of the Federal government’s fiscal contingencies in the fiscal\nframework and are a source of both general and extraordinary revenues in the budget. They\nare:\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1797,
      "end_char": 1973,
      "text": "• Fondo Mexicano de Petróleo para la Estabilización y el Desarrollo (FMP): This is Mexico’s\nsovereign wealth fund. It manages the (non-tax) oil revenues from PEMEX and private\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1973,
      "end_char": 2165,
      "text": "companies in the sector, collecting them and transferring them to the Federal government. The\nfund also executes financial transactions related to exploration and extraction contracts granted\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2165,
      "end_char": 2346,
      "text": "by the government. FMP is required to transfer a share of oil revenues to the Federal\ngovernment for budgetary financing, with the size of the transfer equal to the amount included\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2346,
      "end_char": 2531,
      "text": "in the government’s budget. Thereafter, as stipulated in the Federal Budget and Fiscal\nResponsibility Law (FBFRL), it must invest in Federal- and state-level stabilization funds (up to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2531,
      "end_char": 2723,
      "text": "2.22 and 0.64 percent of projected oil revenues, respectively) before it can accumulate reserves\nin a long-run savings account, the Long-Term Reserves. When oil revenues exceed 4.7 percent of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2723,
      "end_char": 2912,
      "text": "GDP, the excess amount goes directedly to the Long-Term Reserves. Since 2018, reserves have\nbeen roughly equal to USD 1 billion (0.07 percent of GDP in 2022).1 FMP transfers to the Federal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2912,
      "end_char": 3078,
      "text": "government, representing non-tax oil revenues, are expected to be MEX$673 billion (2.3 percent\nof GDP) in 2022, up from MEX$364 billion (1.4 percent of GDP) in 2021.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3078,
      "end_char": 3267,
      "text": "• Fondo de Estabilización de los Ingresos Presupuestarios (FEIP): FEIP is a source of in-year\nfinancing for the budget when revenues fall short of budget projections. The primary source of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3267,
      "end_char": 3352,
      "text": "1 https://www.fmped.org.mx/administracion-reserva.html\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 79"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 86",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 203,
      "text": "MEXICO\na time when risks are concentrated in the near-term. But this approach also entails trade-offs, with\ncomplexity increasing as a result and knock-on consequences for transparency. Moreover, as the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 203,
      "end_char": 401,
      "text": "additional funding for FEIP is predicated on whatever headroom against the debt ceiling is left at\nyear-end, the extent of financing is subject to uncertainty and therefore unreliable. Finally, the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 401,
      "end_char": 565,
      "text": "reforms do not address the pro-cyclical bias in the current fiscal framework resulting from both the\nPSBR and the Balanced Budget Rule (BBR) (see Annex X Table 1).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 565,
      "end_char": 751,
      "text": "8. Longer-run options entail more comprehensive reforms to Mexico’s fiscal framework.\nReforms such as the establishment of a debt anchor, clarification and tightening of definitions and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 751,
      "end_char": 942,
      "text": "procedures, expanded expenditure rule coverage, and a focus on medium-term sustainability\nstrategies would enhance the flexibility of fiscal policy while maintaining and strengthening fiscal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 942,
      "end_char": 1142,
      "text": "responsibility. The table in this annex compares the features of the current fiscal framework with\nstaff proposals along the lines discussed. As these proposals represent a notable departure from the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1142,
      "end_char": 1296,
      "text": "existing framework, a medium-term sustainability strategy (itself a recommendation) could be used\nto anchor the transition.\n82 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 84",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 192,
      "text": "MEXICO\nfinancing for FEIP comes from oil hedging operations, with a technical committee at Mexico’s\nMinistry of Finance (SHCP) determining the volume of oil exports to be covered and a strike\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 192,
      "end_char": 379,
      "text": "price for put options. Strike prices are generally consistent with the assumed oil price in the\nbudget and 210–215 million barrels were hedged in this way between 2010 and 2017. However,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 379,
      "end_char": 563,
      "text": "a secondary source of financing for FEIP comes from transfers from FMP. In 2020, the FEIP\nreceived MEX$47 billion (0.2 percent of GDP) from oil hedging operations, MEX$9 billion (0.04\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 563,
      "end_char": 754,
      "text": "percent of GDP) from FMP transfers, and another MEX$9 billion (0.04 percent of GDP) from\nreturns on its financial assets. Conversely, FEIP transferred a total of MEX$157 billion (0.7 percent\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 754,
      "end_char": 939,
      "text": "of GDP) to the budget due to revenue shortfalls relative to budget projections. The balance of\nthe fund marked MEX$9.5 billion (0.04 percent of GDP) by the end-2020 compared to MEX$158\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 939,
      "end_char": 1137,
      "text": "billion (0.6 percent of GDP) in the previous year. Transfers to the fund equaled MEX$7.5 billion\n(0.01 percent of GDP) in 2021 and are expected to equal MEX$8 billion (0.03 percent of GDP) in\n2022.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1137,
      "end_char": 1323,
      "text": "3. Given prospects and recent patterns, existing financing mechanisms might not allow\nFEIP to be replenished. The windfall oil revenue gains collected by FMP are increasingly being used\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1323,
      "end_char": 1522,
      "text": "by the budget as fuel-based spending pressures have risen, leaving little or no funds to rebuild the\nFEIP balance or build up FMP reserves. Moreover, while the authorities estimate that a balance of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1522,
      "end_char": 1713,
      "text": "around MEX$100 billion in FEIP (0.35 percent of 2022 GDP and 0.33 percent of 2023 GDP) would\nprovide for an adequate fiscal contingency for 2023, the balance in the fund was MEX$25.0 billion\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1713,
      "end_char": 1911,
      "text": "(0.09 percent of 2022 GDP) as of end-June 2022 and the aforementioned expected transfers to the\nfund in 2022 will only provide an additional 0.03 percent of GDP. The starting balance of the fund in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1911,
      "end_char": 2144,
      "text": "2023 could thus fall short of the MEX$100 billion target.\n4. A long-run consideration is whether the oil revenues and the hedging mechanism can\nprovide sufficient resources for counter-cyclical policy in the future. Recent PEMEX oil\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2144,
      "end_char": 2341,
      "text": "production has provided for a smaller oil revenue base, regardless of the oil price, increasing the\nlikelihood of residual funds being too small for replenishing contingencies after they have been\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2341,
      "end_char": 2557,
      "text": "used. Moreover, as the oil hedge is not directly linked to Mexico’s economic cycle, it need not\nfunction as an automatic fiscal stabilizer.\nBudget Support and Oil Revenues, 2018–22\nSources: SHCP, staff calculations.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2557,
      "end_char": 2739,
      "text": "Note: ‘Total Oil Revenues’ covers revenues accruing to the consolidated budget and so includes both Pemex\nrevenues and Federal government oil revenues.\n80 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 85",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 213,
      "text": "MEXICO\nB. Reforms Proposed in the 2023 Budget Package\n5. Reforms to the fiscal framework proposed by the Mexican government seek to\nintroduce new mechanisms for financing FEIP. Reforms to the FRBL proposed in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 213,
      "end_char": 407,
      "text": "government’s 2023 budget package would create a new funding mechanism for FEIP based on debt\nissuance. Under the proposals, where actual debt issuance falls short of that budgeted for the year,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 407,
      "end_char": 601,
      "text": "the government would create an IOU equivalent to the shortfall between actual debt issuance and\nthe debt ceiling in the budget and deposit this in FEIP. In the next fiscal year, FEIP could then\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 601,
      "end_char": 809,
      "text": "dispose of, or exchange, this asset at will to increase its liquidity. Liquidity in this instance need not\nbe cash and the reforms enable FEIP to hold financial assets, though it still cannot incur financial\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 809,
      "end_char": 1015,
      "text": "liabilities.2 In the first instance, assets relate to government paper. However, in principle, FEIP could\nalso hold other instruments. Given that FEIP holds and contracts put options as per the oil hedging\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1015,
      "end_char": 1201,
      "text": "program, some precedent exists regarding FEIP’s ability to conduct and manage financial\ntransactions.\n6. Under the reforms, the trade-off between financing FEIP and addressing immediate\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1201,
      "end_char": 1380,
      "text": "spending concerns is mitigated while diversifying the financing mechanism away from oil\nreduces vulnerability to trends in oil prices and production. Under the current framework,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1380,
      "end_char": 1581,
      "text": "increasing financing for FEIP would likely entail greater transfers from FMP – meaning lower levels of\nfinancing to support the Federal government budget, state-level funds, and FMP’s reserves as well\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1581,
      "end_char": 1778,
      "text": "as alterations to the apportionment methodology – or the exercising of put options on oil.3 On the\nlatter, as these put options can only be exercised when oil prices fall below that assumed in the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1778,
      "end_char": 1968,
      "text": "government budget, this mechanism does not contribute to building contingencies prior to any\nshock as resources flowing into FEIP in this scenario are already earmarked for budget financing\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1968,
      "end_char": 2167,
      "text": "given an oil revenue shortfall. Additionally, the size of the funds available to FEIP, and therefore\navailable for a counter-cyclical policy response, is limited by the amount of oil placed into the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2167,
      "end_char": 2361,
      "text": "hedging mechanism. This places constraints on the financing base available for FEIP in the context\nof the importance of oil revenues in supporting the budget and a downward trend in production.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2361,
      "end_char": 2536,
      "text": "Adding additional mechanisms for financing FEIP, including debt issuance, creates a broader\nfinancing base and one that can be tapped in advance of the realization of shocks.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2536,
      "end_char": 2717,
      "text": "7. The reforms represent a pragmatic step forward in enabling fiscal policy to respond to\nimmediate downside risks, but comprehensive reform is necessary to improve flexibility and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2717,
      "end_char": 2912,
      "text": "reliability while ensuring transparency and fiscal responsibility. The ratification of the proposed\nreforms before the end of 2022 are expected to free up MEX$20–30 billion for FEIP. The reforms\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2912,
      "end_char": 3098,
      "text": "could therefore provide large support to the contingencies required should the downside risks\nfacing the domestic and global economy in 2023 materialize. By seeking to leave many of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 3098,
      "end_char": 3310,
      "text": "parameters of the existing fiscal framework untouched, the reforms enable quick implementation at\n2 The type of financial asset that FEIP would be allowed to hold includes government securities or other types of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3310,
      "end_char": 3486,
      "text": "liquid asset that could be sold, securitized, or collateralized.\n3 A transfer from FMP’s reserves to FEIP is another possibility and would leave budgetary resources untouched.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3486,
      "end_char": 3707,
      "text": "However, this requires that the Chamber of Deputies approve such a transfer. Existing legislation expedites this\ndecision-making process where FMP reserves exceed 3 percent of GDP by the end of the previous year—a remote\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00020",
      "start_char": 3707,
      "end_char": 3929,
      "text": "possibility with reserves standing at 0.07 percent of GDP in July 2022. FMP reserves can be used directly for\nbudgetary needs even when they are below 3 percent of GDP but FEIP resources must be completely exhausted first\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00021",
      "start_char": 3929,
      "end_char": 4030,
      "text": "and a two-thirds majority vote by the Chamber of Deputies is required.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 81"
    }
  ]
}
